 {
  "text": "A roofing contractor is replacing the roof covering on a two-storey school building. The work will take four weeks. The contractor has told workers to use ladders throughout, without setting up scaffold, to save time and money.\n\nExplain why this approach is unsafe and describe a safer method of access the contractor should use instead.",
  "type": "6 marks — Explain",
  "marks": "Mastering (5-6 marks): Explains that using ladders for a four-week roofing project is unsuitable — ladders are only appropriate for short-duration, light work where a three-point contact can be maintained; they do not provide a stable working platform or edge protection for roofing tasks. Identifies the specific risks: falling from the ladder or the roof edge; falling objects landing below; no guardrails or toe-boards protecting workers or people at ground level. Describes a safer method: scaffold with guardrails (handrails and toe-boards) should be erected around the building to give a stable working platform at eaves level; this provides edge protection and prevents falling objects at ground level. Notes that the scaffold must be inspected by a competent person at least every seven days, and that weather conditions must be monitored. Saving time and money does not justify exposing workers to a foreseeable risk of death or serious injury.\nSecure (3-4 marks): Identifies that ladders are unsuitable for this task, names a safer access method and gives one or two safety points.\nDeveloping (2 marks): Identifies the hazard but limited explanation of safer alternatives.\nEmerging (1 mark): A single basic point. [6 marks]"
 },
 {
  "text": "A maintenance plumber is called to repair a gas boiler and a burst water pipe in the same property. Both jobs require working on live services.\n\nEvaluate the safety steps the plumber must take for each job before and during the work, and explain why Gas Safe registration is particularly important for the gas work.",
  "type": "8 marks — Evaluate (Extended Response)",
  "marks": "Mastering (7-8 marks): Evaluates safety steps for the water repair: isolate water supply at the relevant stop valve before work begins; use appropriate PPE (waterproof clothing, safety footwear); check for secondary risks (e.g. water damage to electrical fittings nearby). Evaluates safety steps for the gas repair: must be Gas Safe registered (it is illegal to carry out gas work without this); isolate the gas supply at the meter or isolation valve before starting; follow appropriate working practices and safety procedures for gas; use appropriate PPE; test for gas leaks on completion before restoring supply. Justifies why Gas Safe registration is particularly important: gas leaks can cause explosions, fires and carbon monoxide poisoning — all potentially fatal — and an unregistered person may lack the training to carry out the work safely or to test the system correctly on completion. The requirement for Gas Safe registration exists in law precisely because the consequences of unsafe gas work are catastrophic. A substantiated judgement: both jobs require isolation and PPE, but the gas job carries a higher risk of catastrophic harm, which is why registration and formal accreditation are legally required rather than merely recommended.\nSecure (5-6 marks): Both jobs discussed, Gas Safe explained, some evaluation of relative risk.\nDeveloping (3-4 marks): Relevant points for one or both jobs, mostly descriptive.\nEmerging (1-2 marks): Basic points, little development. [8 marks]"
 }
]

